Z||000003||batboy||11-08-2000||09:48 PM||||I started out with a $1,000 budget for stereo upgrades for my 1998 Grand Prix GT. Don't ask me why, because this car already has the best audio system of any car I've ever had. Haha, how naive I was thinking that I could spend ONLY a grand.<P>I decided to go with Infinity Kappa 50.2 components in the front (5-1/4" mids and 1" tweets) and Infinity Kappa 40.2 components in the rear for fill (4" mids and 1" tweets).<P>Ok, next I bought a couple of new Rockford Fosgate Punch amps that were on sale, one is<BR>rated 30X2 watts rms and the other 25X2. The bigger amp will run the front components<BR>and the smaller one will power the rear components. (By the way, if these amp ratings sound wimpy, think again. R-F amps are very under-rated. The smaller amp has a birth sheet of 202 total watts max!)<P>Oh no! I was already pushing the limit of my $1,000 budget, especially if I still wanted that CD changer. How did that happen? Power blocks, 4 gauge power wire, heavy duty speaker wire, fuses, wire terminals, foam speaker mounting baffles, and RCA cables all added up to nearly $200 and I even had some of the stuff on hand.<P>My buddies told me I HAD to have a good 3 way active crossover and a BIG sub in the trunk. After hearing their agreements, then checking into it, and listening to several competition level car audio systems, I decided to forget about the CD changer for now and go for a sub and crossover. Of course that means buying a big enough amp to run the sub.<P>Suddenly, I'm way over budget again. Oh woe! But...I sold my 85 Fiero GT that I had lost interest in. Even after paying some bills, I still had an extra $500 from that sale to tack onto my Grand Prix stereo "blackhole" project.<P>I settled on an Infinity Perfect 12" sub and a 1.25 cu.ft. sealed box (similar to the Q-Logic only beefier), powered by a bridged R-F Punch 250a amp. Hard to beat that combo! Finally, I choose the MTX LSX2 active crossover that has speaker level inputs for easy painless interfacing with the factory stereo wiring. There is also an optional External Bass Control (ECB) that allows remote dash bass adjustment while still retaining the factory fader controls too.<P>Hehe, guess what? I'm already pushing my new and revised $1,500 budget, however, if I do run over a little it's no big deal. Of course, I still have the "joy" of doing the whole install myself. Nothing like blood, sweat, tears, and cussing in order to save a buck, huh?<P>If I get that Alpine head unit and CD changer I want next summer, that will pop me well over 2 grand spent. So, Phil T... I think your $700 budget is pretty dang cheap.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
